  <main>

    <!-- Abstract -->

    <section class="section white">

      <div class="container narrow">

        <h2>Abstract</h2>

        <div class="rule"></div>

        <p>
          Endoscopic 3D reconstruction supports surgical navigation,
          intraoperative visualization, and scene understanding. Recent
          surgical 3D Gaussian Splatting methods reduce the dependence on
          SfM initialization, but often introduce optical-flow-driven pose
          estimation into the reconstruction loop, coupling camera tracking
          with Gaussian mapping and increasing computational cost.
        </p>

        <p>
          We present <strong>FPGS</strong>, a fixed-geometry Gaussian mapping
          framework for endoscopic surgical scene reconstruction. Camera
          poses and intrinsics are estimated by an upstream geometry module
          and then fixed in the reconstruction backend. A first-frame depth
          prior is used only for Gaussian initialization, while subsequent
          mapping is driven by RGB photometric supervision. Optical-flow
          supervision, projection-flow loss, persistent depth supervision,
          and online pose refinement are removed from the reconstruction
          backend.
        </p>

        <p>
          On nine SCARED scenes, FPGS improves PSNR by
          <strong>2.62 dB</strong> and SSIM by <strong>0.086</strong>, while
          reducing LPIPS by <strong>0.092</strong> compared with Free-SurGS
          under the fixed-geometry setting. Training time is reduced by
          <strong>66.7%</strong>, peak GPU memory by <strong>44.0%</strong>,
          and rendering speed is increased by <strong>33.7%</strong>.
        </p>

        <figure class="main-figure">

          <img
            src="assets/images/problem-setting.webp"
            alt="Comparison between flow-driven reconstruction and FPGS"
          >

          <figcaption>
            Problem setting under different camera-geometry assumptions.
            Flow-driven reconstruction couples pose estimation with Gaussian
            mapping, whereas FPGS uses fixed external geometry.
          </figcaption>

        </figure>

        <p class="keywords">
          <strong>Keywords:</strong>
          Endoscopic scene reconstruction, 3D Gaussian Splatting,
          external geometry, flow-free reconstruction,
          fixed-geometry mapping.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- Motivation -->

    <section class="section gray">

      <div class="container">

        <h2>Motivation &amp; Design Choice</h2>

        <div class="rule"></div>

        <div class="cards">

          <article>

            <h3>Coupled Tracking and Mapping</h3>

            <p>
              Flow-driven pipelines estimate camera motion inside the
              reconstruction loop, coupling pose optimization with Gaussian
              scene optimization.
            </p>

          </article>

          <article>

            <h3>Redundant Geometry Optimization</h3>

            <p>
              When stable external poses and calibrated intrinsics are
              already available, repeatedly optimizing camera geometry may
              be unnecessary.
            </p>

          </article>

          <article>

            <h3>Persistent Auxiliary Supervision</h3>

            <p>
              Optical flow, projection flow, and depth supervision enlarge
              the optimization loop and increase memory and preprocessing
              cost.
            </p>

          </article>

          <article>

            <h3>Clinical Engineering Setting</h3>

            <p>
              Robot kinematics, external tracking, or upstream geometry
              estimation can provide fixed camera geometry to the
              reconstruction backend.
            </p>

          </article>

        </div>

        <div class="statement">

          <strong>Research question.</strong>

          When stable external geometry is available, is flow-driven online
          pose optimization still necessary for the endoscopic 3DGS
          reconstruction backend?

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- Framework -->

    <section class="section white">

      <div class="container">

        <h2>FPGS Framework</h2>

        <div class="rule"></div>

        <figure class="main-figure full">

          <img
            src="assets/images/method-overview.webp"
            alt="Overview of the FPGS framework"
          >

          <figcaption>
            Overview of FPGS. External camera geometry is fixed, the
            first-frame depth prior is used only for initialization, and
            subsequent reconstruction is performed by RGB-only Gaussian
            mapping.
          </figcaption>

        </figure>

        <div class="method-text">

          <p>
            <strong>Camera geometry estimation.</strong>
            Camera poses and intrinsics are obtained from an upstream
            geometry module. They are treated as fixed inputs and are not
            included in the optimization variables of the reconstruction
            backend.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>First-frame initialization.</strong>
            The first-frame depth prior is back-projected with the fixed
            camera parameters to initialize Gaussian centers and appearance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>RGB-only mapping.</strong>
            After initialization, only Gaussian position, scale, rotation,
            opacity, and appearance are optimized using RGB photometric
            supervision.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Global refinement.</strong>
            Sampled frames refine the Gaussian scene while keeping poses and
            intrinsics fixed. Tracking, flow-related losses, and depth
            supervision are not reintroduced.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- Fixed-geometry optimization -->

    <section class="section gray">

      <div class="container">

        <h2>Fixed-Geometry RGB-Only Optimization</h2>

        <div class="rule"></div>

